    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5b010655_e4d2c9df",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-30T19:37:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yup, we can keep trying to discover the cable nDiscoverIdentityCount times.\n\nIf PD 2.0 times out, it doesn\u0027t hurt to re-set to PD 2.0.  I wouldn\u0027t expect a cable to hit this condition too often normally (it\u0027s a cable... how much else does it really have to do besides reply to SOP\u0027 discovery?) but the worst-case would be that we only have PD 2.0 discovery information for what was actually a misbehaving PD 3.0 partner.  Since PD 3.0 provides us with collision avoidance, we should hopefully not often time out with an actual PD 3.0 port partner.",
      "parentUuid": "3f488b73_b33fcdb9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f4759a30_98b6e79c",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-30T22:33:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well by clearing the TIMEOUT flag all the time aren\u0027t we actually prevent PD_DISC_FAIL from getting set in the _exit transition?\n\nAlso, I would vote for using 3 of the retries on PDF 3.0 and the remain on 2.0 (or if we think that is too complicated at least call out that we think it is worth giving up PD 30 after one attempt and try 5 times with PD 2.0)",
      "parentUuid": "5b010655_e4d2c9df",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b5356cf4_77b8f625",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-04-30T22:40:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Cable discovery failure should come from the fact that we\u0027ve met or exceeded our counter immediately below.\n\nIf we wanted to be more precise and we had the GoodCRC packet to analyze, we could set the revision based on the partner\u0027s GoodCRC revision, but I don\u0027t think we can get that from the TCPC, can we?\n\nOtherwise, I\u0027m not sure which is best.  All my cables are to be PD 2.0 and have no issue responding correctly to a PD 3.0 request. Nathan, any opinions on how long to try PD 3.0 before falling back?",
      "parentUuid": "f4759a30_98b6e79c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8dee791b_63c8a052",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-02T22:57:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e If we wanted to be more precise and we had the GoodCRC packet to analyze, we could set the revision based on the partner\u0027s GoodCRC revision, but I don\u0027t think we can get that from the TCPC, can we?\n\nThe Revision field in a GoodCRC packet is not meaningful information. From the PD3.0 spec (6.2.1.1.5 Specification Revision):\n\n\u003e \"The 2-bit Specification Revision field of a GoodCRC Message does not carry any meaning and Shall be considered as \"don’t care\" by the recipient of the Message.\"\n\n\u003e Also, I would vote for using 3 of the retries on PDF 3.0 and the remain on 2.0 (or if we think that is too complicated at least call out that we think it is worth giving up PD 30 after one attempt and try 5 times with PD 2.0)\n\nRegarding this, some eMarkers take a while to \"boot up\". Theoretically, a slow-booting eMarker may not be online for the PD3.0 messages, then will \"just so happen\" due to timing issues to wake up for PD2.0.\n\nIt\u0027s a race condition/collision thing, so not sure how to best handle that.\n\nLet me check go/wwcb-library for if we have any commercially-available PD3.0 eMarker cable DUT for you, and grok this comment a bit more.",
      "parentUuid": "b5356cf4_77b8f625",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d9cdc0a3_ed27c48e",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-02T23:06:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Clarifying: the UFP/DFP role in a GoodCRC is /also/ somewhat meaningless information. From PD3.0 (6.2.1.1.6 Port Data Role):\n\n\u003e The Port Data Role field Shall only be defined for SOP Packets. For all other SOP* Packets the Port Data Role field is Reserved and Shall be set to zero.\n\u003e \n\u003e If a USB Type-C® Port receives a Message with the Port Data Role field set to the same Data Role as its current Data Role, except for the GoodCRC Message, USB Type-C Error Recovery actions as defined in [USB Type-C 2.0] Shall be performed.\n\nHowever, this didn\u0027t stop particular DUTs (b/132285896#comment46 ) (b/116340006#comment7 ) from freaking out when it received a GoodCRC with the wrong \"role bit\" set.\n\nThus we should carefully contemplate how to follow spec... while being \"tolerant\" of bad DUT.",
      "parentUuid": "8dee791b_63c8a052",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "70e27382_8b156193",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-02T23:22:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e We\u0027d have to think through the other scenarios that could cause a timeout with GoodCRC sent.  Are there any in particular you\u0027re worried about?\n\nThere is one explicit state for Cable SOP\u0027 where GoodCRC is sent, but no reply: duplicate MessageID headers, which should ordinarily lead to a (SOP\u0027 in this case) Soft Reset to resynchronize counters.\n\n\u003e For subsequent Messages, if MessageID value in a received Message is the same as the stored value, the receiver Shall return a GoodCRC Message with that MessageID value and drop the Message (this is a retry of an already received Message).\n\u003e \n\u003e Note: this Shall Not apply to the Soft_Reset Message which always has a MessageID value of zero.\n\nConsidering further, we should carefully whiteboard this in PD3.0 (8.3.3.24.3 Source Startup Structured VDM Discover Identity of a Cable Plug State Diagram).\n\nSee also PD3.0 (6.2.1.1.5 Specification Revision). This EXPLICITLY deals with our question here.\n\n\u003e Prior to entering an explicit contract, the VCONN Source Shall use the following steps to establish a Specification Revision level:\n\u003e \n\u003e (...)\n\u003e \n\u003e b)When a Cable Plug does not respond to a Revision 3.0 Discover Identity REQ with a Discover Identity ACK or BUSY the VCONN Source May repeat steps 1-4 using a Revision 2.0 Discover Identity REQ in step 1 before establishing that there is no Cable Plug to communicate with.\n\nTechnically, a GoodCRC response WITHOUT and ACK is (b). Bending the rules here...",
      "parentUuid": "d9cdc0a3_ed27c48e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b59d1b45_e226e301",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-04T17:45:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For cable plugs which take a while to \"boot up\", would you expect them to GoodCRC without replying?  If they don\u0027t GoodCRC, we\u0027ll continue to re-try PD 3.0 in this scenario because we\u0027ll hit the protocol error case.\n\nFor the duplicate message ID headers causing a soft reset, can you point me to the spec section for that one?  I\u0027m not having luck finding it in my spec-spelunking this morning. \n\nNice find!  So per-spec we \"may\" retry with PD 2.0.  I\u0027ll add that reference into the code comments.",
      "parentUuid": "70e27382_8b156193",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6dcda9f3_bd3fec9c",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-04T20:02:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e For the duplicate message ID headers causing a soft reset, can you point me to the spec section for that one?  I\u0027m not having luck finding it in my spec-spelunking this morning. \n\nLikely covered under (6.8.1 Soft Reset and Protocol Error). Duplicate header ID messages \u003d GoodCRC is sent, but no reply. Thus Soft_Reset process \"opens up\" to us. (Important note: Until we get a GoodCRC, Soft_Reset is closed off to us -- since we can\u0027t be sure plug is PD-capable in the first place.)\n\nIgnore the bit about having left PE_SRC_RDY. We aren\u0027t dealing with \"an unexpected packet\"... but instead \"protocol failure\" which falls into the [[MAY]] statement below.\n\nAlso helpful citations:\n\n* PRL_Tx_Check_RetryCounter state diagram in (Figure 6-54 Common Protocol Layer Message Transmission State Diagram).\n* (8.3.3.24.2.3 DFP/VCONN Source SOP’/SOP’’ Soft Reset or Cable Reset of a Cable Plug or VPD State) which implies SOP\u0027 Soft_Reset can only be used after \"Previously Cable Discovered\" -- OR -- request from DPM.\n* (8.3.3.24.3 Source Startup Structured VDM Discover Identity of a Cable Plug State Diagram) says \"Device Policy Manager is notified\" on VDMResponseTimer timeout. Thus it\u0027s a policy choice at that point.\n\n\n\u003e The Soft Reset [[May]] be triggered by either Port Partner in response to the Protocol Error.\n\u003e (...)\n\u003e A failure to see a GoodCRC Message in response to any Message within tReceive (after nRetryCount retries), when a Port Pair is Connected, is indicative of a communications failure resulting in a Soft Reset (see Section 6.6.9.1).\n\u003e (...)\n\u003e The sender of a Soft_Reset Message Shall reset its MessageIDCounter and RetryCounter, the receiver of the Message Shall reset its MessageIDCounter and RetryCounter before sending the Accept Message response.\n\u003e (...)\n\u003e Any failure in the Soft Reset process will trigger [a Hard Reset when SOP Packets are being used] or [Cable Reset, sent by the DFP only, for any other SOP* Packets[; for example a GoodCRC Message is not received during the Soft Reset process (see Section 6.8.3 and Section 6.8.4).\n\nI added brackets for clarity in the last paragraph. Please note a \"Cable Reset\" (SOP\u0027 special bitstream) is separate and distinct from a \"Soft Reset\" (PD message).\n\n\u003e 6.8.4 Cable Reset\n\u003e \n\u003e Only a DFP Shall generate Cable Reset Signaling. [[A DFP Shall only generate Cable Reset Signaling within an Explicit Contract.]] A Cable Reset Shall cause all Active Modes in the Cable Plugs to be exited (see Section 6.4.4.3.4).\n\ntl;dr:\n\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n(1) If we get PD3.0 GoodCRC back but no response (VDMResponseTimer timeout), we are Cable_NAK\u0027d state. Policy layer can Soft_Reset the cable, downrev [since \"NAK\u0027ed\u0027], and try again. We can even proceed to SOP\u0027 Cable_Reset hard signaling if needed if SOP\u0027 Soft_Reset fails.\n\n(2) If we get nRetryCount transmit failure (no GoodCRC), we are (Discover Identity request sending Failure). Same Cable_NAK\u0027d state, DPM notified. Policy manager MAY downrev PD version and try again. Since no GoodCRC, we can\u0027t be sure PD-capable eMarker exists so Soft_Reset is off the table.\n\nIf we don\u0027t want to break spec by cutting (RetryCounter) in half (to avoid Sink HARD_RESET\u0027ing us for SRC_CAP timeout)... we need to interleave SRC_CAP with DiscID.",
      "parentUuid": "b59d1b45_e226e301",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2f89d6c0_a4256aaa",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-06T22:24:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We should already be interleaving SRC_CAP with DiscID, by virtue of always prioritizing the SRC_CAP timer.\n\nFor 1, how would we tell that we got a PD 3.0 GoodCRC if the GoodCRC Revision field is useless?  \n\nFor 2, I don\u0027t think we could down-rev after reaching nDiscoverIdentityCount given this language in 6.6.15 DiscoverIdentityTimer:\n\n\"If no GoodCRC Message response is received after nDiscoverIdentityCount\nDiscover Identity Command requests have been sent by a Port, the Port Shall Not send any further SOP’/SOP’’\"\n\nWe\u0027d have to pick a point to down-rev before then.  \n\nBased on the wording you found in the Specification Revision section of the message header, I would guess the down-rev is geared towards a case with a GoodCRC received (since it mentions not getting an ACK back, as opposed to message sending failure).  But it\u0027s definitely not stated explicitly.",
      "parentUuid": "6dcda9f3_bd3fec9c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "59a9a0fc_969f5fe4",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-06T23:45:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e For 1, how would we tell that we got a PD 3.0 GoodCRC if the GoodCRC Revision field is useless?  \n\nI apologize for being imprecise. By \"PD3.0 GoodCRC\" I meant \"We sent a PD3.0 DiscID and get a GoodCRC back\".\n\nPer PD3.0 (6.2.1.1.5 Specification Revision) we cannot tell what partner is from GoodCRC alone. When responding to inbound packet with (01b \u003d PD2.0) set, the receiver [SHALL] set (01b) in GoodCRC. [This includes us! AI: We should verify this.] If responding to inbound packet with (10b \u003d PD3.0) receiver [MAY] set (00b 01b or 10b).\n\nThus, the person getting the GoodCRC back can\u0027t tell anything from (01b). (00b or 10b) are *most likely* a PD3.0 DUT. (01b) could be anything. But spec says \"Don\u0027t Care\" so we should honor that -- in case there\u0027s something in PD4.0+ spec that exploits the Ignore in state machine.\n\n\n\u003e For 2, I don\u0027t think we could down-rev after reaching nDiscoverIdentityCount given this language in 6.6.15 DiscoverIdentityTimer: (...)\n\nFound the \"eMarker boot time\" citation: tVCONNStable. Good catch. I think that restriction on  nDiscoverIdentityCount doesn\u0027t apply because we\u0027re not in an explicit contract.... yet.\n\n\u003e (6.4.4.3.1 Discover Identity)\n\u003e Cable Plug will not be ready for PD Communication until tVCONNStable after VCONN has been applied (see [USB Type-C 2.0]).\n\u003e \n\u003e During Cable Plug discovery, [[when there is an Explicit Contract,]] Discover Identity Commands are sent at a rate defined by the DiscoverIdentityTimer (see Section 6.6.15) up to a maximum of nDiscoverIdentityCount times (see Section 6.7.5).\n\n\u003e (6.6.15 DiscoverIdentityTimer)\n\u003e The DiscoverIdentityTimer is used [[during an Explicit Contract]] using SOP’. (...)\n\nBefore connect, we\u0027re in an Implicit Contract. Still probing for Src_Cap response to become Explicit. And \"just so happen\" to be trying to un-bork the eMarker.",
      "parentUuid": "2f89d6c0_a4256aaa",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4185,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 4187,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "1f139d55152a79671ffceb34b6e86e88a7c0bb8a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
